Backfilling is essentially a time machine for your data pipelines. 
It allows you to force an orchestrator like Airflow to look backward and execute a job for dates in the past,
rather than just ticking forward on its normal schedule.

Technical Breakdown :- 
Airflow pipelines are heavily driven by the concept of a logical_date (formerly execution_date). 
When a DAG runs, Airflow injects this timestamp into the tasks. A backfill is triggered—usually via the 
CLI command airflow dags backfill—by providing a historical start and end date. 
Airflow then mathematically calculates every scheduled interval (like every day or every hour) 
that occurred within that window. It spawns a distinct DAG run for each interval, 
passing the specific historical logical date into the execution environment so your scripts pull exactly that day's
slice of data.

Buisness Context :- 
This is critical for two major business scenarios: Disaster Recovery and Feature Engineering. 
If an upstream API silently failed and dropped data for two weeks, we need to backfill to recover those 
missing records. Alternatively, if the business defines a brand new KPI today, executives can't wait a year 
to see year-over-year trends. We backfill the pipeline to retroactively calculate that new KPI across the 
last three years of raw historical data.

Design Decision :-
The most devastating mistake junior engineers make with backfills is writing SQL that 
relies on database functions like CURRENT_DATE() or CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(). 
If you launch a backfill for the year 2023 today, every single historical run will evaluate to today's date,
completely destroying your table partitions. As a senior engineer, I enforce strict CI/CD checks to ensure all 
date filters are parameterized using Airflow's Jinja macros, like {{ ds }}. 
This guarantees that when Airflow simulates running on October 1st, the SQL query explicitly filters for October 1st.
